---
title: A cube, from three.js
description: A module installed in mkdoc, imported by a document.
---

<script>
    import { onMount } from 'svelte';
    import * as THREE from 'three';

    let canvas;

    onMount(() => {
        const width = canvas.clientWidth;
        const height = canvas.clientHeight;

        const scene = new THREE.Scene();
        const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(50, width / height, 0.1, 100);
        camera.position.set(2.4, 1.8, 2.4);
        camera.lookAt(0, 0, 0);

        const cube = new THREE.Mesh(
            new THREE.BoxGeometry(),
            new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({ color: 0x6688cc, roughness: 0.45 }),
        );
        scene.add(cube);
        scene.add(new THREE.AmbientLight(0xffffff, 1.4));

        const key = new THREE.DirectionalLight(0xffffff, 2.2);
        key.position.set(3, 4, 2);
        scene.add(key);

        const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ canvas, antialias: true, alpha: true });
        renderer.setPixelRatio(Math.min(devicePixelRatio, 2));
        renderer.setSize(width, height, false);

        let frame;
        const draw = (time) => {
            cube.rotation.x = time / 2600;
            cube.rotation.y = time / 1700;
            renderer.render(scene, camera);
            frame = requestAnimationFrame(draw);
        };
        frame = requestAnimationFrame(draw);

        // The dev server swaps this component in place on every save, so the
        // previous run's loop and its GPU context have to go with it.
        return () => {
            cancelAnimationFrame(frame);
            renderer.dispose();
        };
    });
</script>

<canvas bind:this={canvas} width="640" height="360" style="width: 100%; height: auto;"></canvas>

The cube above is drawn by [three.js](https://threejs.org), which is not
installed anywhere near this document. It is installed in mkdoc, and listed
under `mkdoc.modules` in mkdoc's own `package.json`:

```json
{
    "mkdoc": {
        "modules": ["three"]
    }
}
```

That is the whole arrangement. A document imports the module by name, the way it
would in a project that had one:

```svelte
<script>
    import * as THREE from 'three';
</script>
```

Nothing is bundled for being on the list. A document that never mentions three
still builds to a page with no JavaScript in it at all --- the list only decides
what a document is *allowed* to reach for.

The `<script>` block is also what makes this page ship its bundle: mkdoc assumes
a document with one has something to run, and hydrates it. `hydrate: false` in
the frontmatter would leave the canvas blank.

## Building it

A hydrated page needs to be served over HTTP --- browsers refuse to load ES
modules from `file://` --- so this one does not open off the filesystem the way
a static page does:

```sh
mkdoc build example/scene.svx
python3 -m http.server -d build/scene
```
